1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,400
Sometime in the mid-1940s,

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:07,767
an engineer named Percy Spencer reached
into his pocket and found a mess.

3
00:00:08,033 --> 00:00:10,000
His candy bar had melted.

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:11,367
That wouldn't be strange —

5
00:00:11,367 --> 00:00:13,300
except for where he'd been standing.

6
00:00:13,567 --> 00:00:15,367
Right next to a magnetron:

7
00:00:15,367 --> 00:00:17,033
the heart of a radar set,

8
00:00:17,033 --> 00:00:19,333
the part that makes invisible waves.

9
00:00:19,633 --> 00:00:24,600
So how does a melted snack in a radar lab
turn into the box on your kitchen counter?

10
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:26,967
What actually happens when you press Start?

11
00:00:26,967 --> 00:00:31,033
And why does your food come out scalding
in one spot and cold in the next?

12
00:00:33,533 --> 00:00:36,267
Spencer wasn't a typical scientist.

13
00:00:36,267 --> 00:00:38,100
He left school at about 12,

14
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:40,133
learned radio in the Navy,

15
00:00:40,133 --> 00:00:42,233
and taught himself most of the rest.

16
00:00:42,533 --> 00:00:43,567
During the war,

17
00:00:43,567 --> 00:00:46,100
his production methods helped Raytheon build

18
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:48,967
radar magnetrons by the thousands every day.

19
00:00:49,267 --> 00:00:51,167
So when his candy bar melted,

20
00:00:51,167 --> 00:00:52,500
he didn't just shrug.

21
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:53,700
He started testing.

22
00:00:53,967 --> 00:00:56,767
He set some popcorn
kernels near the magnetron.

23
00:00:56,767 --> 00:00:57,767
They popped.

24
00:00:58,633 --> 00:00:59,833
He tried an egg.

25
00:00:59,833 --> 00:01:00,933
It exploded.

26
00:01:01,633 --> 00:01:04,033
Then came the step that mattered most.

27
00:01:04,033 --> 00:01:06,133
Instead of letting the energy spread out,

28
00:01:06,133 --> 00:01:08,500
he concentrated it into a small space.

29
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:10,767
Food placed there heated fast.

30
00:01:11,667 --> 00:01:14,033
In October, 1945,

31
00:01:14,033 --> 00:01:17,800
Raytheon filed a patent
for cooking food with microwaves.

32
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:21,433
People had tried heating food
with radio energy before,

33
00:01:21,433 --> 00:01:23,133
but with much longer waves.

34
00:01:23,433 --> 00:01:25,800
Spencer's idea was to go short —

35
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:28,067
waves about 10 centimeters long,

36
00:01:28,067 --> 00:01:30,233
close to the size of the food itself.

37
00:01:30,933 --> 00:01:32,367
A couple of years later,

38
00:01:32,367 --> 00:01:35,000
the first microwave oven went on sale.

39
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,800
It was called the Radarange.

40
00:01:37,333 --> 00:01:39,633
It stood nearly six feet tall,

41
00:01:39,633 --> 00:01:42,533
and weighed about as much
as four grown adults.

42
00:01:42,533 --> 00:01:45,933
Its magnetron needed water
piped in to keep it cool.

43
00:01:45,933 --> 00:01:47,867
And it cost thousands of dollars —

44
00:01:47,867 --> 00:01:49,467
so it went to restaurants,

45
00:01:49,467 --> 00:01:50,467
not homes.

46
00:01:51,367 --> 00:01:52,667
Twenty years later,

47
00:01:52,667 --> 00:01:54,600
in 1967,

48
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:57,533
a countertop model arrived for home kitchens.

49
00:01:57,833 --> 00:02:00,000
$495 —

50
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,267
still pricey, but no longer
a restaurant machine.

51
00:02:03,267 --> 00:02:05,600
And small enough to sit next to a toaster.

52
00:02:06,733 --> 00:02:08,900
Even then, it took a while.

53
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:10,467
But by the late nineties,

54
00:02:10,467 --> 00:02:13,300
about nine in ten American homes had one.

55
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:15,300
The machine shrank.

56
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:17,533
The idea inside it stayed the same.

57
00:02:17,533 --> 00:02:19,033
So let's look inside.

58
00:02:19,933 --> 00:02:22,133
Press Start, and a magnetron —

59
00:02:22,133 --> 00:02:24,467
a descendant of Spencer's radar tube —

60
00:02:24,467 --> 00:02:27,133
turns electricity into microwaves.

61
00:02:27,133 --> 00:02:29,100
They're a kind of radio wave.

62
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:32,933
The metal walls bounce them
around until the whole box is full of them.

63
00:02:33,867 --> 00:02:37,867
A microwave is a ripple of electric
and magnetic fields.

64
00:02:37,867 --> 00:02:40,833
The part that matters here
is the electric field.

65
00:02:40,833 --> 00:02:43,067
It pushes on electric charges —

66
00:02:43,067 --> 00:02:47,567
and it swings back and forth about
2.5 billion times every second.

67
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:51,467
Most of the food we heat
contains plenty of water.

68
00:02:51,467 --> 00:02:53,733
And a water molecule is lopsided.

69
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:56,700
The oxygen end is slightly negative,

70
00:02:56,700 --> 00:02:59,467
the hydrogen side slightly positive.

71
00:02:59,467 --> 00:03:01,433
So every time the field flips,

72
00:03:01,433 --> 00:03:03,300
it shoves the molecule one way —

73
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:04,300
then the other.

74
00:03:04,733 --> 00:03:06,533
Here's a picture that helps.

75
00:03:06,533 --> 00:03:08,633
Imagine a packed dance floor,

76
00:03:08,633 --> 00:03:11,500
where the beat reverses
billions of times a second.

77
00:03:11,500 --> 00:03:13,433
Everyone tries to turn with it.

78
00:03:13,433 --> 00:03:15,867
Nobody can — it's far too crowded.

79
00:03:15,867 --> 00:03:17,600
So they bump into their neighbors,

80
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:19,000
who bump into theirs,

81
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,700
until the whole floor is a jostling mess.

82
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:24,233
That's only a picture.

83
00:03:24,233 --> 00:03:27,633
Real molecules don't spin
like tiny propellers.

84
00:03:27,633 --> 00:03:28,967
They're jammed together,

85
00:03:28,967 --> 00:03:32,600
and they barely manage part of a turn
before the field reverses.

86
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:34,500
But the key idea holds.

87
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:35,967
The field keeps shoving,

88
00:03:35,967 --> 00:03:39,333
and collisions scatter
that shove into random jiggling.

89
00:03:39,333 --> 00:03:41,333
And random jiggling of molecules —

90
00:03:41,333 --> 00:03:42,600
that's what heat is.

91
00:03:44,133 --> 00:03:45,533
A quick myth check.

92
00:03:45,533 --> 00:03:48,933
This isn't a magic frequency
that water is tuned to.

93
00:03:48,933 --> 00:03:51,933
It's simply a frequency set aside for ovens.

94
00:03:51,933 --> 00:03:54,633
And water isn't the only thing soaking it up.

95
00:03:54,633 --> 00:03:56,467
Other molecules in food absorb some,

96
00:03:56,467 --> 00:03:57,467
too.

97
00:03:58,100 --> 00:04:01,733
One more: microwaves don't
cook from the inside out.

98
00:04:01,733 --> 00:04:03,233
In a thick bowl of soup,

99
00:04:03,233 --> 00:04:05,933
they fade out within
the first few centimeters.

100
00:04:05,933 --> 00:04:08,400
The middle warms the old-fashioned way —

101
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:11,133
heat spreading
in from the hotter outer layers.

102
00:04:12,233 --> 00:04:14,100
Which brings us to the turntable.

103
00:04:14,633 --> 00:04:17,100
Remember the waves bouncing off the walls?

104
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:18,300
Where they overlap,

105
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:20,767
some spots add up and get stronger.

106
00:04:20,767 --> 00:04:23,067
Others cancel out and get weaker.

107
00:04:23,067 --> 00:04:27,367
The result is a pattern of hot
and cold spots that mostly stays put.

108
00:04:28,467 --> 00:04:30,167
You can see it with —

109
00:04:30,167 --> 00:04:31,700
fittingly — a bar of chocolate.

110
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:33,133
Take out the turntable,

111
00:04:33,133 --> 00:04:35,100
put in a plain chocolate bar,

112
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:36,400
and heat it briefly.

113
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:38,000
It doesn't melt evenly.

114
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:39,500
It melts in patches,

115
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:41,133
a few centimeters apart.

116
00:04:43,033 --> 00:04:44,933
That's what the turntable is for.

117
00:04:44,933 --> 00:04:48,100
Picture one spoonful of soup
riding around the plate.

118
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:50,733
It drifts into a strong spot and heats up,

119
00:04:50,733 --> 00:04:52,433
slides through a weak one,

120
00:04:52,433 --> 00:04:54,033
then into another strong one —

121
00:04:54,033 --> 00:04:55,400
again and again.

122
00:04:55,733 --> 00:04:57,233
Turn after turn,

123
00:04:57,233 --> 00:04:58,867
the heating averages out.

124
00:05:01,167 --> 00:05:02,067
Averages out —

125
00:05:02,067 --> 00:05:03,600
but not perfectly.

126
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:05,867
The real pattern is three-dimensional,

127
00:05:05,867 --> 00:05:08,100
and it shifts as the food heats.

128
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:11,000
And the very center of the plate
barely moves at all.

129
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,500
That's why food-safety guides still say:

130
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:14,867
stir halfway through.

131
00:05:16,400 --> 00:05:19,167
And then there's the classic
microwave betrayal.

132
00:05:19,167 --> 00:05:20,800
A plate too hot to touch —

133
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:22,500
and a bite that's still cold.

134
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:24,900
Part of that is the food.

135
00:05:24,900 --> 00:05:26,533
The outside heats first,

136
00:05:26,533 --> 00:05:29,033
the middle waits for heat to creep inward,

137
00:05:29,033 --> 00:05:29,933
and the plate,

138
00:05:29,933 --> 00:05:31,733
pressed against the hot outside,

139
00:05:31,733 --> 00:05:33,067
soaks up plenty.

140
00:05:33,367 --> 00:05:37,033
A few minutes of standing time lets
the heat keep spreading inward.

141
00:05:37,733 --> 00:05:39,267
Part of it can be the dish.

142
00:05:39,267 --> 00:05:42,233
Some dishes let microwaves
pass straight through,

143
00:05:42,233 --> 00:05:44,400
and only get warm from the food.

144
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:46,767
Others absorb the energy themselves.

145
00:05:46,767 --> 00:05:48,333
Here's a simple test.

146
00:05:48,333 --> 00:05:50,600
Put the empty dish in next to a cup of water,

147
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:52,000
and run it for one minute.

148
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,033
Hot water and a cool dish?

149
00:05:54,033 --> 00:05:55,133
It's fine.

150
00:05:55,133 --> 00:05:56,367
If the dish is hot,

151
00:05:56,367 --> 00:05:57,967
it isn't microwave-safe.

152
00:05:58,933 --> 00:06:01,200
Frozen food has its own trick.

153
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:03,733
Ice is bad at absorbing microwaves —

154
00:06:03,733 --> 00:06:06,133
its molecules are locked in place.

155
00:06:06,133 --> 00:06:08,800
But once one patch thaws into liquid water,

156
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:11,700
it soaks up far more energy
than the ice around it.

157
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,400
So it heats faster,

158
00:06:13,400 --> 00:06:14,733
and faster still —

159
00:06:14,733 --> 00:06:18,733
which is how one bite can be scalding
while the next is frozen solid.

160
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:21,433
And one rare surprise.

161
00:06:21,433 --> 00:06:24,867
Plain water, heated alone in a very clean cup,

162
00:06:24,867 --> 00:06:27,900
can get hotter than boiling
without a single bubble.

163
00:06:27,900 --> 00:06:29,033
Then a small bump —

164
00:06:29,033 --> 00:06:30,833
and it can erupt all at once.

165
00:06:32,767 --> 00:06:34,500
So the next time you press Start,

166
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:36,300
picture what's really going on.

167
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:41,700
A descendant of a wartime radar tube
fills a metal box with invisible waves.

168
00:06:41,967 --> 00:06:45,800
Their electric field swings
back and forth billions of times a second,

169
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:48,333
shoving at water molecules
too crowded to keep up.

170
00:06:48,333 --> 00:06:50,833
That jostling becomes heat,

171
00:06:50,833 --> 00:06:51,300
spot by spot and layer by layer,

172
00:06:51,300 --> 00:06:56,267
while the plate carries your dinner
through a pattern you can't see.

173
00:06:57,200 --> 00:07:00,000
Nothing in your food turns radioactive.

174
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:01,300
It's just waves,

175
00:07:01,300 --> 00:07:04,167
water, and a very crowded dance floor —

176
00:07:04,167 --> 00:07:07,233
all because one engineer
found a melted candy bar,

177
00:07:07,533 --> 00:07:08,900
and wanted to know why.
